
# ======================== CQRS ========================
Your reads are 100x heavier than your writes, How would you design it ?
Before saying "increase the read replicas", ask if the read and writes are of the same shape, then this might not be the best answer the answer to this is CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)

Without CQRS

Suppose you're building an e-commerce application.

Your database looks like this:

Users
Orders
OrderItems
Products
Payments
Addresses
Coupons

When someone opens Order History, your query might be:

SELECT ...
FROM orders
JOIN users
JOIN order_items
JOIN products
JOIN payments
JOIN addresses
JOIN coupons
...

That's a heavy query.

Now imagine 10 million users are viewing their order history.

Every request executes those joins.

With CQRS

The write side stays exactly the same.

Orders
OrderItems
Products
Payments

Whenever an order changes:

Create Order
      │
      ▼
Write Database

Instead of making every reader perform joins, you prepare a read model.

Order Summary

orderId
customerName
total
status
productNames
estimatedDelivery

This table is already denormalized.

Now your query becomes:

SELECT *
FROM order_summary
WHERE orderId = ?

No joins.

No aggregations.

No calculations.

The expensive work was done once, when the order changed—not every time someone views it.


Complete example 
Step 1: User creates an order

The write database stores normalized data.

Orders
--------
id = 101
userId = 5
status = PAID

OrderItems
-----------
orderId = 101
productId = 7
productId = 12

Products
---------
7 -> iPhone
12 -> Charger

Users
------
5 -> Vikas

Everything is stored correctly.

Step 2: Publish an event

After saving the order:

await Order.create(...);

eventBus.publish({
    type: "OrderCreated",
    orderId: 101
});

Instead of saying:

"Update order_summary"

you say:

"An order was created."

Anyone interested can react.

Step 3: Event handler updates the read model

Another service (or another part of your app) listens:

eventBus.on("OrderCreated", async (event) => {

    const order = await Order.findById(event.orderId)
        .populate("user")
        .populate("items.product");

    await OrderSummary.create({
        orderId: order.id,
        customerName: order.user.name,
        total: order.total,
        products: order.items.map(i => i.product.name),
        status: order.status
    });

});

Now your read table contains

OrderSummary

orderId    customerName    total    products
------------------------------------------------
101        Vikas           1200     iPhone, Charger

Notice there are no joins when reading anymore.

Reading becomes simple

Instead of

Orders
JOIN Users
JOIN Products
JOIN OrderItems
JOIN Payments
JOIN Coupons

you do

SELECT *
FROM order_summary
WHERE orderId = 101;

Very fast.

What if the order changes?

Suppose

PAID
↓

SHIPPED

The write side updates:

await Order.update(...);

eventBus.publish({
    type: "OrderShipped",
    orderId: 101
});

Listener:

eventBus.on("OrderShipped", async (event) => {

    await OrderSummary.updateOne(
        { orderId: event.orderId },
        { status: "SHIPPED" }
    );

});

Now

OrderSummary

101
Status = SHIPPED

is updated automatically.